DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Each file system type known to the kernel has a _v_f_s_c_o_n_f structure that
     contains the information required to create a new mount of that file sys‐
     tems type.

     struct vfsconf {
             struct  vfsops *vfc_vfsops;     /* file system operations vector */
             char    vfc_name[MFSNAMELEN];   /* file system type name */
             int     vfc_typenum;            /* historic file system type number */
             int     vfc_refcount;           /* number mounted of this type */
             int     vfc_flags;              /* permanent flags */
             struct  vfsconf *vfc_next;      /* next in list */
     };

     When a new file system is mounted, mount(2) does a lookup of the _v_f_s_c_o_n_f
     structure by its name, and if it is not already registered, attempts to
     load a kernel module for it.  The file system operations for the new
     mount point are taken from _v_f_c___v_f_s_o_p_s, and _m_n_t___v_f_c in the _m_o_u_n_t structure
     is made to point directly at the _v_f_s_c_o_n_f structure for the file system
     type.  The file system type number is taken from _v_f_c___t_y_p_e_n_u_m which was
     assigned in vvffss__rreeggiisstteerr(), and the mount flags are taken from a mask of
     _v_f_c___f_l_a_g_s.  Each time a file system of a given type is mounted,
     _v_f_c___r_e_f_c_o_u_n_t is incremented.

     vvffss__rreeggiisstteerr() takes a new _v_f_s_c_o_n_f structure and adds it to the list of
     existing file systems.  If the type has not already been registered, it
     is initialized by calling the vvffss__iinniitt() function in the file system op‐
     erations vector.  vvffss__rreeggiisstteerr() also updates the oid's of any sysctl
     nodes for this file system type to be the same as the newly assigned type
     number.

     vvffss__uunnrreeggiisstteerr() unlinks _v_f_c from the list of registered file system
     types if there are currently no mounted instances.  If the vvffss__uunniinniitt()
     function in the file systems initialization vector is defined, it is
     called.

     vvffss__mmooddeevveenntt() is registered by VVFFSS__SSEETT() to handle the loading and un‐
     loading of file system kernel modules.  In the case of MOD_LOAD,
     vvffss__rreeggiisstteerr() is called.  In the case of MOD_UNLOAD, vvffss__uunnrreeggiisstteerr() is
     called.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     vvffss__rreeggiisstteerr() returns 0 if successful; otherwise, EEXIST is returned in‐
     dicating that the file system type has already been registered.

     vvffss__uunnrreeggiisstteerr() returns 0 if successful.  If no _v_f_s_c_o_n_f entry can be
     found matching the name in _v_f_c, EINVAL is returned.  If the reference
     count of mounted instances of the file system type is not zero, EBUSY is
     returned.  If vvffss__uunniinniitt() is called, any errors it returns will be re‐
     turned by vvffss__uunnrreeggiisstteerr().

     vvffss__mmooddeevveenntt() returns the result of the call to vvffss__rreeggiisstteerr() or
     vvffss__uunnrreeggiisstteerr(), whatever the case.

